{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"Contradiction requires mutual incompatibility on the same run, but the target concedes SRC_009's threshold rule operates on bounded integer weights while SRC_008's check is weight-blind. A weight-blind check is simply silent on weight-dependent predicates; it neither validates nor invalidates them. The claimed divergence—enrichment flips a weight-<τ attacker to accepted—shows SRC_009's weight-sensitive guarantee can fail in a situation SRC_008's check does not address, but that is scope orthogonality, not contradiction. For a genuine contradiction the same run must be simultaneously certified by SRC_008 and rejected by SRC_009 under a shared decision criterion; the target never establishes that SRC_008's label-based check asserts the root valid (it may merely abstain), so the two foundations need not disagree on the same run.","target":"2063113e5f436fe093e4df641548b9f254c79296d8869ccbf9e1522d5406d2a3"},{"attack":true,"case":"The reduction claim says SRC_011's validity guarantee is a corollary of SRC_008's 'no new accepted attacker on a root' check and that the library is exactly a policy for choosing one admissible enrichment. But the target's own refutation condition admits a possible case family requiring an edge incident to a committed root—outside SRC_008's allowed enrichment domain. If such a case exists, the guarantee cannot be a corollary of a check that does not govern that domain, so the reduction is not established for the full library; it holds at most for the restricted subfamily. A reduction that silently excludes part of the reduced system is not a faithful reduction.","target":"4fb338de4d4e75410d29010d51f0f0a2e083bb14e329c7e611395d5e08d61f7d"},{"attack":true,"case":"The abstraction claims all three transformations share the single load-bearing constraint 'no accepted attacker reaches a committed root' and are provenance-blind. But 'no accepted attacker reaches' is a semantic grounded-extension property, while the target simultaneously classifies the three as pre-fixpoint syntactic expansions whose only load-bearing constraint is syntactic (no generated edge incident to a committed root). A syntactic non-incidence condition does not entail the semantic no-accepted-attacker condition unless an additional soundness argument bridges fixpoint semantics to syntax, which the target never provides. The abstraction conflates two distinct constraint kinds under one label, so the shared mechanism is not substantively demonstrated.","target":"8549107c74c738d8fb406d628148408bb9b58befe943dae2cac07573d1a6afda"}]}